<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">German East Africa</h1><p class="st-meta">1885 CE – 1919 CE · capital Dar es Salaam</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Germany's largest colony, covering mainland Tanzania, Rwanda and Burundi, the scene of the Maji Maji war and a long First World War campaign.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1884 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters">Carl Peters</a> made treaties with chiefs inland from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Zanzibar</a> coast, and in 1885 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa-company">German East Africa Company</a> received an imperial charter. The coastal Abushiri revolt of 1888 to 1889 against the company forced the German state to intervene under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-von-wissmann">Hermann von Wissmann</a>, and in 1891 the territory became a crown colony with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>German rule relied on military stations, African soldiers (askari), coastal officials called akida and taxes paid in cash or labour. Settlers and plantations grew sisal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, and the Central Railway linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>. Forced cotton growing provoked the Maji Maji rebellion of 1905 to 1907, crushed with famine tactics; estimates of the dead range from about 75,000 to 300,000.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-lettow-vorbeck">Paul von Lettow-Vorbeck's</a> forces fought a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> the colony was divided: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> received <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tanganyika-territory">Tanganyika Territory</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Belgium</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ruanda-urundi">Ruanda-Urundi</a> and Portugal the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kionga-triangle">Kionga Triangle</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/german-east-africa.jpg" alt="German East Africa" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Imperial Colonial Office of the German Empire Vector: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichskolonialflagge.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1885 CE – 1919 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dar es Salaam</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hermann von Wissmann, Heinrich Schnee</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Islam, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tanzania">Tanzania</a>, <a href="/countries/rwanda">Rwanda</a>, <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Deutsch-Ostafrika, German East Africa Company territory</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1902&amp;focus=german-east-africa" aria-label="German East Africa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5767 2432 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where German East Africa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6087 2688L5857 2500M6087 2688L5826 2522M6087 2688L5827 2571M6087 2688L5830 2535M6087 2688L6063 2617M6087 2688L5937 2568M6087 2688L5985 2725M6087 2688L5300 622M6087 2688L6087 2688"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5857 2500h0M5826 2522h0M5827 2571h0M5830 2535h0M6063 2617h0M5937 2568h0M5985 2725h0M5300 622h0M6087 2688h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6087 2688h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6087 2688h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dar es Salaam. <a href="/atlas?year=1902&amp;focus=german-east-africa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
